course: dictionary
lesson: 466
title: Arnica: a flower and a cream
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one the writer means. «Jake's mum keeps a box just for him: plasters, arnica cream (a cream for bruises) and a bag of frozen peas.» The brackets explain arnica cream, and it sits in a box with plasters and frozen peas, so it is something for hurts.
explain: Arnica has two meanings. It is a yellow flower that grows in mountains and is related to daisies. It is also a cream or gel made from that plant, which some people rub on bruises and sore muscles. Look at the words around it to decide which meaning fits.
explain: Arnica is for the skin, not for swallowing. The plant is poisonous if eaten. Notice the phrases people use with it: «arnica cream», «arnica gel» and «rub on arnica».
fact: Arnica is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some arnica, not two arnicas.
fact: «Arnica» means a yellow flower that grows in mountains, related to daisies.
fact: «Arnica» can also mean a cream or gel made from that plant, used on bruises and sore muscles.
fact: Arnica is poisonous if eaten, so it must never be swallowed.
fact: Useful phrases: «arnica cream», «arnica gel», «rub on arnica».
exercise: «Jake's mum keeps a box just for him: plasters, arnica cream (a cream for bruises) and a bag of frozen peas.» What is arnica cream, according to the brackets? | A kind of bread | A bag for school | *A cream for bruises | A yellow flower on a hill
exercise: Arnica is related to which plant? | A tree | *A daisy | A cabbage | A cactus
exercise: Why must arnica never be swallowed? | It tastes of frozen peas | It only works on hair | *It is poisonous if eaten | It makes you sleepy
test: In «rub on arnica», what does arnica mean? | A bruise | *A cream or gel for the skin | A mountain | A kind of food
test: Which phrase means a cream made from the plant? | A yellow daisy garden | A mountain walk | *arnica gel | A box of plasters
exam: Which is the safest way to use arnica? | Eat the yellow petals | Swallow a spoonful for a sore head | Put it in your drink | *Rub a little on a bruise
